    "updated_at": "2024-05-21T06:47:02Z",
    "body": "> Looking at this again I wonder how Firefox defeats the attack. I was misunderstanding something initially. What does Firefox never collect? Maybe @asutherland can help out here.\r\n\r\nIn Firefox, all shipped MessagePorts go through our IPC.  When our IPC layer is processing the implicit close for the collected port:\r\n- We [decide to close the entangled port](https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/rev/00b18126ec6a14a710086eb24e6a281460a678d5/dom/messagechannel/MessagePortService.cpp#303)\r\n- Which then results in us [calling close on the parent IPC actor](https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/rev/00b18126ec6a14a710086eb24e6a281460a678d5/dom/messagechannel/MessagePortService.cpp#274).\r\n- But our [Close method just dissociates the parent actor from our service](https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/rev/00b18126ec6a14a710086eb24e6a281460a678d5/dom/messagechannel/MessagePortParent.cpp#158-161) but does not tear down the IPC parent actor, so the child actor never knows that it's not talking to anyone.  We [do have a method that would do that](https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/rev/00b18126ec6a14a710086eb24e6a281460a678d5/dom/messagechannel/MessagePortParent.cpp#153-156) but we [only call that in the nightmarish case where the port was further re-shipped but there are messages in-flight and state machines are catching up with that](https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/rev/00b18126ec6a14a710086eb24e6a281460a678d5/dom/messagechannel/MessagePortService.cpp#281).\r\n\r\nHonestly, it's not clear this behavior was entirely intentional (we will certainly spew NS_WARNINGs in the parent process when this case happens if the closed port sends us messages); the code has a bunch of technical debt and is due for a cleanup.  But I think I might have avoided changing the behavior in a few bugs because it seemed like it might have been equally been intentional to avoid leaking GC, but it's hard to tell because our infrastructure makes it hard to find old discussion of things.",
